For instance, if you have a set of variables x1, x2, ..., xn, you can use a for loop to execute a block of code on each variable:

```cpp
for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    // Code to be executed on variable xi
    cout << "x" << i << " = " << xi << endl;
}
```

Alternatively, you can use the `std::apply` function from the `<functional>` header to execute a lambda function on a tuple of variables. This approach is useful when you have a fixed-size set of variables and want to avoid the overhead of a loop.

```cpp
std::tuple<double, double, double> variables = {x1, x2, x3};
std::apply([](auto... args) {
    // Code to be executed on the variables
    std::cout << "x1 = " << args[0] << std::endl;
    std::cout << "x2 = " << args[1] << std::endl;
    std::cout << "x3 = " << args[2] << std::endl;
}, variables);
```

Another option is to use the `std::invoke` function, which allows you to invoke a function (including a lambda) on multiple arguments.

```cpp
double x1 = 5, x2 = 10, x3 = 15;
std::invoke([](double x) { std::cout << "x = " << x << std::endl; }, x1, x2, x3);
```

